- How would you reconstruct all k blocks from the backpointers?
- How might you optimize to O(nk) space or prune inner loop with bit tricks?

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem combines prefix DP with an inner backward sweep to compute the optimal segment ending at each position. It tests understanding of state decomposition, reconstruction logic, and handles edge cases like negative values naturally.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: dp[i][t] tracks the optimal value using the first i elements with t segments
- Inner loop: expanding the left boundary computes segment maximum on the fly
- Reconstruction: store predecessor pointers to recover exact subarray boundaries
- Complexity: O(n²k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueSubarrays(A, k) {
    const n = A.length;
    const dp = Array(n + 1).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
    const prev = Array(n + 1).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    dp[0][0] = 0;

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
            let curMax = -Infinity;
            for (let l = i; l >= 1; l--) {
                curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[l - 1]);
                const val = (dp[l - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity ? dp[l - 1][t - 1] + curMax : -Infinity);
                if (val > dp[i][t]) {
                    dp[i][t] = val;
                    prev[i][t] = l;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    const result = [];
    let i = n, t = k;
    while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
        const l = prev[i][t];
        result.unshift([l, i]);
        i = l - 1;
        t--;
    }
    return { value: dp[n][k], boundaries: result };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with an extra resource dimension (shields) on a classic grid path. Demonstrates handling of constrained resources, 3D DP states, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP with an additional dimension for resources (k)
- Transition with/without consuming a shield
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGridPath(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(NEG)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    for (let j=0; j<m; j++) {
      for (let k=0; k<=K; k++) {
        const v = grid[i][j];
        if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k] + v);
        if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k] + v);
        if (k>0 && v < 0) {
          if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k-1]);
          if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k-1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(...dp[n-1][m-1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover one optimal path and its moves?
- How to optimize space to O(n*m*K)?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates DP on selecting exact number of non-overlapping subarrays with reconstruction, common in profit/segmentation tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarrays
- Exact k selections
- Prefix sums for efficient range sums
- Backpointers for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length; const pre = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) pre[i] = pre[i-1]+A[i-1];
  const neg inf = -1e18;
  let dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> new Array(k+1).fill(neg_inf));
  let best = new Array(k+1).fill(neg_inf);
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++) best[t] = neg_inf;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      best[t] = Math.max(best[t], dp[i-1][t-1] - pre[i-1]);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], pre[i] + best[t]);
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for at-most-k windows instead of exactly k?
- How would you reconstruct the actual windows efficiently and handle negative values?

## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces a new DP angle: exact k segments, per-segment cost, and sum-based values, requiring both interval DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- DP over i (prefix length) and t (blocks)
- Reconstructing endpoints from choice pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(A,k,c){
  const n=A.length;
  const P=[0];
  for(const x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1]+x);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const pos=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1]=(P[i]-P[0])-c, pos[i][1]=0;
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let s=t-1;s<=i-1;s++){
        const val=dp[s][t-1]+(P[i]-P[s])-c;
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; pos[i][t]=s; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  let i=n, t=k;
  const blocks=[];
  while(t>0){
    const s=pos[i][t];
    blocks.push([s, i-1]);
    i=s; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {score: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP on strings, palindrome precomputation, and reconstruction. It also requires handling a non-trivial scoring function beyond simple lengths.

## Key Concepts

- Palindrome table isPal[i][j] for substring s[i..j]
- dist[i][j] stores number of distinct characters in s[i..j]
- DP state dp[i][t]: best score for first i chars partitioned into t palindromes
- Transition: for all j < i with isPal[j][i-1], dp[i][t] = max(dp[i][t], dp[j][t-1] + dist[j][i-1])
- Reconstruction via parent pointers to recover cut positions
- Time/space: O(n^2 k) time and O(n^2 k) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPalPartitionScore(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const isPal = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(false));
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { isPal[i][i] = true; dist[i][i] = 1; }
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      if (s[i] === s[j] && (len === 2 || isPal[i+1][j-1])) isPal[i][j] = true;
      const seen = new Set();
      for (let p = i; p <= j; p++) seen.add(s[p]);
      dist[i][j] = seen.size;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        if (isPal[j][i-1] && dp[j][t-1] > -Infinity) {
          const cand = dp[j][t-1] + dist[j][i-1];
          if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; par[i][t] = j; }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    cuts.push([j, i]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return { score: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize memory with rolling DP when n is large?
- How does the approach adapt if the score uses a different function, e.g., length of palindrome minus distinct count?
- What are the practical limits for n and k for this to run within time constraints?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates dynamic programming expertise across string partitioning with fixed segment counts, numeric parsing constraints, and solution reconstruction—fundamental skills for real-world interview scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over string prefixes and partition counts
- Handling substring numeric values with leading-zero validation
- Solution path reconstruction for determining optimal cut positions
- Time and space complexity optimization techniques

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;

  // Helper to get numeric value of substring [l, r)
  const getValue = (l, r) => {
    if (s[l] === '0' && r - l > 1) return null; // leading zero
    return Number(s.slice(l, r));
  };

  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let j = t - 1; j < i; j++) {
        const val = getValue(j, i);
        if (val !== null && dp[j][t-1] + val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[j][t-1] + val;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0) {
    const prev = parent[curr][currT];
    cuts.push(prev);
    curr = prev;
    currT--;
  }
  cuts.pop(); // remove 0
  cuts.reverse();

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Notes

- The DP explores all possible cut positions while respecting the k-substring constraint
- Leading zero validation is crucial for correctness
- The parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal cuts

Why This Is Asked

Tests DP for non-overlapping intervals with fixed cost and gaps, reflecting scheduling problems.

Key Concepts

- DP with dp[i][t] for first i days and t windows
- Prefix sums for quick window sums
- End-at-i transitions with a preceding gap
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(nk)

Code Example

```javascript
function maxProfitKWindows(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prv = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) {
    for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) {
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
      if (t>0) {
        for (let s=1;s<=i;s++) {
          if (i- s >= 2) {
            const val = dp[s-2][t-1] + (S[i]-S[s-1]) - c;
            if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; prv[i][t] = s; }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k], endIndex: n, t: k};
}
```

